WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:02.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: A typical
customer service experience

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.800
requires multiple
conversational twists

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.860
and turns to achieve
the required outcome.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.950
Stick around to learn how
Dialogflow CX simplifies

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.840
a complex conversational
experience.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.326
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.240
Hi.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.450
I am Priyanka Vergadia, and in
this episode of "Deconstructing

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:26.460
Chatbots," we will help
our friends at PUggs

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.090
understand exactly
how a Dialogflow CX

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:33.710
conversation is defined and
visualized as a state machine.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.840
Like any e-commerce
company, PUggs

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.960
has customers that call in
for ordering cute dog shoes,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.360
and for returning
or exchanging them.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.520
As you can imagine,
each of these topics

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.330
require multiple conversational
turns for the virtual agent

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:51.230
to acquire necessary
information for that customer.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.090
That's where flows come in.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.030
Flows are used to
contain and control

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.570
the definition of these
topics and their associated

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:01.230
conversational paths.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.390
Here's how this works.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.930
Every agent is automatically
created with one flow,

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.060
called Default Start Flow.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.250
Sometimes this single
flow may be all

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.560
you need for a simple agent.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.950
Look at this example.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.840
Default Start Flow here provides
store hours, store location,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.730
and helps place new orders.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.980
But PUggs' case is a
bit more complicated.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.270
They have different development
teams handling orders, returns,

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.320
and exchanges.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.520
For situations like this,
agents require additional flows.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.680
In this case, the
Default Start Flow

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.110
which is automatically
created for the agent

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.650
leads to the three additional
flows for orders, returns,

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:40.880
and exchanges.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.700
So the three teams can
manage them independently

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.970
and use the Default Start
Flow as a single entry point

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.620
into the conversation.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.960
Now, if we double-click
on any flow,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.080
we find that it's a collection
of pages which represent

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.090
the state of the CX session.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.320
At any given moment, exactly
one page is the current page,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.660
and the current page
is considered active.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.060
And the flow associated
with that page

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.010
is considered active as well.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.949
Every flow has a
special Start page.

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:12.620
When a flow initially
becomes active,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.050
the Start page becomes
the current page.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.710
And depending on where the
user is in the conversation,

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:22.190
the current page will either
stay the same or transition

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.780
to another page.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.910
Now let's understand pages.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.730
Think of pages in
CX as web pages.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.400
When we fill the fields
in the form on a web page

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.710
and submit it, we receive
a corresponding response.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.640
Similarly, a page
in CX has a form

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.150
that is configured to collect
information or parameters

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.590
from the end users.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.080
This information is
relevant to the conversation

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.970
state on that page.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.010
Transition routes
decide where we

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:52.160
want to route the user
from the current page.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.510
Now, let's see this in our PUggs
use case in the CX console.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.450
I've created an agent already
to explain these concepts.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.150
But don't worry, we will
be creating this agent

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.970
from scratch in
the next episode.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.100
For now, if we go
in the Order flow,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.650
we see the Start page,
which will become active

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.780
when the Order flow
triggers and routes

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.000
to the Collect
Information page, which

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.330
collects the parameters
shoe size and the color,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.180
and routes the customer
to the Confirmation page.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.120
If the user confirms, we end
the flow with the End Flow page.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.170
And if the user
does not confirm,

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.100
then the page routes them back
to the Collect Information page

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.180
to capture those parameters
of shoe sizes and the color.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.550
For more details on the
lifecycle of a page,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.950
I've added the links
in the description.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.020
The transition
routes would depend

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.450
on intents, which
represents something

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:49.010
our users want to do during a
conversation with our agent.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:51.860
For example, here in
PUggs' order flow,

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.520
the Start page has a transition
route to Order intent,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.620
where we expect the
user to provide us

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.890
the size and the color of
the shoe they want to buy.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:04.820
Here we also add the multiple
training example phrases

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:08.030
for what customers
might type or say.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.640
Depending on what
they say, the intents

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.730
are matched appropriately
and the parameter values

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:17.240
are extracted, such as the
size and the color of the shoe.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.700
Now, if you've used
Dialogflow Essentials,

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.700
then I want to point out
here that the intents in CX

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.630
have been simplified to make
them more reusable resource.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.430
Going back to the
parameters, they

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.410
have a name and entity type.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:34.490
Entities are used to control
how data from the end user input

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:35.600
is extracted.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.080
So here, the @sys.color
is a system entity

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.500
that refers to color, and
@size is a custom entity

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.630
that refers to the shoe size.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.100
Now, back to the
page for a second.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.700
If the transition route from
the page leads to a form

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.340
where we collect some
parameters from the user,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.040
we need to either do something
with these parameters

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.260
or provide the user
with a response.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.470
In PUggs' case, we
call the Order intent

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.900
and have the user
provide us with the size

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:04.550
and color of the shoes.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.710
Then in the Collect
Information page,

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:10.340
we check the condition, if both
of these pieces of information

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:14.870
are collected, and only then we
route to the Confirmation page.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.540
Until then, we stay
on the page to get

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.310
those pieces of information.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.160
On the Confirmation page,
we actually get back

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.860
to the user using
the fulfillment.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.010
Here I've added the static
text, but this would ideally

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:31.700
be a dynamic text based on the
information coming from PUggs'

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.780
back end through a webhook.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:34.430
All right.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:38.510
So that was a quick summary of
how pages and transitions work

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.540
in Dialogflow CX, along
with a glimpse of the PUggs

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.070
virtual agent.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.490
Now that all the foundational
information is out of the way,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.830
I am excited for the
next episode, where

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.560
we will start building
the PUggs conversational

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.840
experience in Dialogflow CX.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.330
Until then, comment
below to let me know

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.070
your thoughts on this episode.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:01.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

